## The two from the PlayStation Store

Fortnite and MLB The Show 25 are not sold on Steam, and Epic's own store
refuses a script (403), so both come from the PlayStation Store, where each
game's publisher uploads its art. The URLs are images of each game's concept
page (`store.playstation.com/en-us/concept/228748` for Fortnite,
`.../concept/10011213` for MLB The Show 25): the `SIXTEEN_BY_NINE_BANNER`
for `wide`, asked for at `?w=460`, which the image service answers at
504 x 284, and the `PORTRAIT_BANNER` (2:3) for `tall`, asked for at `?w=600`,
which it answers at 764 x 1146 and the script scales to 600 x 900. Each tall
picture is from the same set as its wide one (the same `rnd/<date>/<time>`
folder), so the two agree. Fortnite's art is its CURRENT season's and Epic
replaces it every season; the files here are the ones fetched on the dates
in the table, and a new season's pictures are the same lookup again.

**VALORANT** (D476, 2026-10-24, the owner asked for its art): not on Steam either, so the
same: concept `10010048`. Its page offers no `SIXTEEN_BY_NINE_BANNER`; `wide` is its
`GAMEHUB_COVER_ART` (16:9, the current Champions key art, without the title; the app writes
the game's name beside it), `tall` its `PORTRAIT_BANNER` (with the title), `panel` its
`MASTER` (1:1, with the title). Riot replaces them with each event.

## The panel pictures (D434)

The owner, 2026-10-22: "make the UI look better with the artwork; find
better artwork for the games if needed that will fit that aspect ratio". The
simple settings menu (D433) draws the config's picture across everything
under its four doors: about 365 CSS pixels wide and 230 to 420 tall on a
phone, `object-fit: cover`, anchored at the top, with the EXO and console
chips across its top (about 70 px) and the config's name across its foot
(about 110 px). A 2:3 cover cut to that box lost its title, so a game may
name a third picture, `panel`: a 900 x 900 JPEG cropped from the
publisher's own art so the title sits whole in the band between the two
banners. A pack without one falls back to its tall picture, then its wide
one (game-packs.js, FALLBACK.panel).

Six of the seven come from the PlayStation Store's image service (Warzone's comes from callofduty.com, below), the same official
source as Fortnite's two pictures above: each is an image the publisher
uploaded to the game's own concept or product page
(`store.playstation.com/en-us/concept/<id>` or `.../product/<id>`), read
from that page's media list on 2026-10-22. `MASTER` is the store's square
key art (1024 x 1024), `SIXTEEN_BY_NINE_BANNER` and `FOUR_BY_THREE_BANNER`
its 3840 x 2160 and 2880 x 2160 banners. These are crops, so
`fetch-pack-art.ps1` does not read this table; the crop column says exactly
what was cut, as `x, y, side` in the source's own pixels, and each square was
then scaled to 900 x 900 and saved at JPEG quality 85. A negative `y` means
the crop starts above the picture: only on Black Ops II and Rust, whose key
art is pure black (RGB 0-5) along its whole top edge, and the rows added are
that same black, so the title sits below the chips.

Warzone's panel (2026-10-23, the third try: the store's green zombie-season
banner, then callofduty.com's daylight Urzikstan key art, were both too
bright for the dark app) is Activision's own: the share image callofduty.com
used for Warzone's Black Ops 7 Season 02 page (`WZ_S2_meta_thumbnail.webp`,
1920 x 1080: a dark red-and-black season key art, one operator with her rifle
over a huge close-up of her eyes, the full CALL OF DUTY WARZONE logo in
white). Its logo sits on the art's bottom edge, so no crop of the art alone
lifts it above the name. The whole picture, unchanged and uncut, is placed at
the top of a 1920 square (x 0, y 0, side 1920); the 840 rows below it are
the same art blurred and darkened, the fill the app already draws behind
off-shape pictures, and the picture's last 60 rows fade into it. The logo
then lands at 45-55 % of the square's height, in the band between the chips
and the name, and the fill sits under the name's dark fade. No pixel of the
art itself is re-toned.

